# Finalise Commands

- Map `finalise` to `npm run finalise`.
- Map `finalise full` to `npm run finalise:full`.
- Map `finalise and push` and `fap` / `/fap` to `npm run finalise:push`.
- Map `finalise full and push` and `ffap` / `/ffap` to `npm run finalise:full:push`.
- Use `npm run finalise:push` or `npm run finalise:full:push` only when the user wrote an authorized push phrase (`finalise and push`, `fap` / `/fap`, `finalise full and push`, `ffap` / `/ffap`, `finalise:push`, or `push to GitHub` as applicable). These aliases implement the global COMPLETE_AND_RELEASE contract and must not be redefined as local-only.
- Before any push, report the branch, commits, and changed files that will be pushed.
- Check for an existing finalise process before starting another.
- Never push after a failed build, test, cleanup, commit, or release-metadata step.
- Read the generated `docs_private/automation/runs/finalise/*.md` summary and report its local path.
- Treat a post-product-commit release failure as fail-closed: preserve local work, do not push, and follow the printed recovery command.
- Local commit is not release-ready, and release-ready is not a push.
- Run `npm run workflow-protocol -- status --blocking` before mutating finalise. Valid split ancestors and exhausted predecessor generations with an owner-authorized successor are historical. Ordinary split/new IDs do not reset review budget. `successor-authorize` may create a fresh owner-authorized generation in the same checkout/branch and must carry unresolved blockers. Review evidence stays bound to its reviewed Git HEAD and working-tree fingerprint. Dry-run is non-mutating. Do not hand-edit protocol JSON.
- Premium DIRECT/TEE-LIGHT continuation authorization may release old V2.4 workflow locks without rewriting failed historical evidence; normal finalise verification and every genuine safety rule still apply.
- Never create or require a branch/worktree merely to reset review state. Optional topology changes require explicit user approval.
